# Step 7 — Capture state

Three separate files. Each has its own update rule.

## 7a — `last_brief.md` + dated archive (two writes per run)

**Write 1 — Rolling state (overwrite each run):**
Save `{{MEMORY_DIR}}/last_brief.md` with:
- Send timestamp
- Fires flagged (for tracking resolution tomorrow)
- Slipped items (for next-day search)
- Priority threads with draft status
- Team commitment statuses ({{DIRECT_REPORT_1}} + {{DIRECT_REPORT_2}})
- Key product signals
- Upcoming deadlines

End with a single line: `*(Board → see delivered.md | Fire calibrations → see calibrations.md)*`

**Write 2 — Dated archive (create, never overwrite):**
Write the identical content to `memory/history/brief-YYYY-MM-DD.md` (substituting today's date). This file is **write-once** — if it already exists from a re-run, skip it. Never modify existing history files.

This is the raw source layer: it lets you reconstruct exactly what Alfred saw and flagged on any given day. `last_brief.md` is the fast-access rolling state; `history/` is the searchable archive.

## 7b — `delivered.md` (append-only archive — never overwrite existing entries)

File: `{{MEMORY_DIR}}/delivered.md`

Alfred writes here during Step 3a (board processing) when Done cards are archived. No additional writes needed in Step 7. The file is append-only — never remove entries.

Archive format (appended by Alfred during Step 3a board processing):
```
## Week of {Mon date}

### {Card title}
{Card description}
*Moved to Done: {date}*
*Impact: {one-line signal from Slack/Granola}* (omit if no signal found)
```

## 7c — `calibrations.md` (permanent, append-only — never overwrite, only add)

File: `{{MEMORY_DIR}}/calibrations.md`

Only write to this file if explicit calibration feedback was given during this run (in chat, Slack reply, or 🚫 react). Append the new rule under `## Active calibrations` in this format:
```
- [{date}] NOT A FIRE: {pattern}
  Rule: {what Alfred should do instead}
  Source: {how this was signaled}
```
If no new calibrations this run, do not touch this file.

## 7d — Run log (prepend entry to `~/alfred-run-log.md`)

Prepend a new entry at the top of the log (below the header, above the previous entry). Keep it tight — this is a machine-readable audit trail.

Format:
```
## {YYYY-MM-DD} ~{HH:MM} AM/PM ET

**Fires:** {count}
{- one line per fire title}

**Resolved since last brief:** {count}
{- one line per resolved item}

**Drafts created:** {count}
{- channel or person name per draft}

**Board movements:**
{- → Archived to delivered.md: {titles}}
{- → New To-Do suggested: {titles}}
{(omit if no movements)}

**Data sources:** {source} ✅/⚠️/❌ · ...

**Days to rollout:** {n}
```

## 7f — Update person entity nodes (append-only)

File pattern: `{{MEMORY_DIR}}/people/{slug}.md`

For each person who appeared in today's **fires, priority threads, key decisions, or team commitments**, append a dated signal entry to their node file. **Never rewrite or remove existing entries — append only.**

**What to append under `## Signal log`:**
```
### YYYY-MM-DD
- [fire|decision|commit-given|commit-received|thread|meeting|shipped|no-signal] One-line description
```

**Update `## Open commitments`** when status changes:
- New commitment surfaced → add `- [ ] {what} — due: {when or "no date"}` under the correct subsection
- Commitment confirmed resolved → change `[ ]` to `[x]` and add `✓ resolved YYYY-MM-DD`

**Create the file if it doesn't exist** using this template:
```markdown
---
name: {Full Name}
role: {Title}
priority: {always-respond | director-plus | team | partner}
tone: {one-line communication note}
company: {slug from memory/companies/ — or blank if internal/unknown}
aliases: [{First}, {Last}, {any known shorthand}]
email: {slack email if known}
---

## Open commitments
### They owe you
*(none)*

### You owe them
*(none)*

